00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.330
So before we dive into the
architectural patterns,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.200
let's take a step back and
look at the big building

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.560
blocks for most end-to-end
real-time stream processing

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:37.820
applications.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.700
So at the very left,
we have the sources

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.070
of data, the applications
that are generating

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:46.340
the continuous streams of data
that we will be processing.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.640
The first thing you want to do
is take those streams of data

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.240
and write them to a
reliable, scalable, high

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.430
throughput streaming
data channel.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:59.240
Think of this as the big pipes
through which your streaming

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.830
data moves.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:04.060
And the two most common
options are Apache Kafka,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.380
which is a popular open
source option, which

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.000
is available on Google Cloud.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.160
And then the other
option is Pub/Sub,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.910
which is Google's fully managed
and serverless offering.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.070
Well, after you've
ingested the data,

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.090
often, that data is going to
be in raw format, not a format

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.410
that is amenable for analysis.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.050
So you want to
take that raw data

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.870
and transform that
data into a format that

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.690
is much better for analysis.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.620
And you do that in real-time
stream processing engines.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.600
And on Google Cloud, the
two most common options,

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.150
the recommended options, are
either Dataproc or Dataflow.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.290
So Dataproc is Google's
managed offering

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.080
for the popular open
source distributed

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.820
processing engine Apache Spark.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.320
Dataflow is Google's cloud
native solution for both stream

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:55.360
and batch processing,
and Dataflow

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.540
is a fully managed and
serverless offering.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.470
Well, so after you've
transformed the data,

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.410
now it's ready to be analyzed.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.620
You can do the analysis in
Dataflow or Dataproc itself.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.520
Both offer fairly flexible
programmatic SDKs.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.430
So you can do arbitrary
analysis and processing

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.350
within Dataflow or Dataproc.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.070
But another common pattern is
you take the transform data,

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.350
and you write it into BigQuery.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:28.080
BigQuery is infinitely scalable,
fully-managed, SQL-based data

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.850
warehouse in the cloud.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.190
So once the data is
written to BigQuery,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.551
you can issue SQL queries to
BigQuery and analyze the data.

00:04:36.551 --> 00:04:37.050
So great.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.030
At this point, we have
analyzed the data,

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.260
and you have likely derived the
insight that is of interest.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.200
But the whole point of
real-time stream processing

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:50.010
is to make that insight, or
the results of our analysis,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.020
actionable in real time.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:56.520
That invariably means there
has to be some end user facing

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:00.540
application where the insight
or the results of the analysis

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:01.710
are served.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.070
And to do that, we write
the analysis, the systems,

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.800
to data stores that
can serve the data.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.200
Common options here
are NoSQL stores,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.360
such as Bigtable and Datastore.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.840
Cloud Spanner is a
transactional database

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.480
that is increasingly becoming
popular for stream processing

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:19.905
use cases.

00:05:19.905 --> 00:05:22.030
And, of course, you can
write the data to BigQuery,

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.380
because when a SQL query
is issued to BigQuery, say

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.750
through the JDBC client, it
immediately serves the result.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.530
So you need some system to serve
the results to the end user

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:35.040
applications, and that
happens to BigQuery, Bigtable,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:35.957
Datastore, or Spanner.

00:05:35.957 --> 00:05:37.289
There are other options as well.

00:05:37.289 --> 00:05:39.460
You can use Cloud SQL,
and so on, and so forth.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.360
But in stream processing,
these four options listed here

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.340
tend to be the common options.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.250
Now, I do want to highlight
that on Google Cloud,

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.520
these tools are
seamlessly integrated

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.190
so that you can focus on
your application logic.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:55.680
And the APIs are neatly
integrated for you

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.260
to transfer data from
one system to the other,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.375
to read data and write data
from one system to the other.

00:06:01.375 --> 00:06:03.000
And the other thing
I want to highlight

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.330
is most of these tools are
managed, and some of them

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:07.040
are serverless.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.200
And what I mean by managed
and serverless is a managed

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.270
offering is one
where you as a user

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.540
do not have to worry about
spinning up clusters, managing

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.150
clusters, managing VMs,
and scaling up and down.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.779
And serverless
offerings are where

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:22.320
you can just forget that there's
a cluster in the background.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.940
You just submit your workload.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:26.790
We take care of spinning
up VMs on your behalf,

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:28.600
scaling them up and
down, and so forth.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.600
So it's really compelling,
because these are all

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.724
seamlessly integrated, and
many of them are managed

00:06:32.724 --> 00:06:33.850
and serverless.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:40.410
So with that, let's dive
into some specific patterns.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.840
But first, let me
set some context.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.152
For simplicity, when I
talk about a pattern,

00:06:45.152 --> 00:06:46.860
I will have an
architectural diagram that

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.410
just focuses on the pattern.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.120
And just to keep
things consistent,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.050
we'll focus on using Pub/Sub
as our default streaming data

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.630
channel, and we'll use
Dataflow as our default stream

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.330
processing tool of choice.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.445
I do want to highlight that
in real world use cases,

00:07:03.445 --> 00:07:05.820
you will actually be combining
a bunch of these patterns.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.260
But over here, just
for simplicity,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.580
so when you look at an
architectural diagram,

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.470
it's easy to read,
I'll just highlight

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.580
one pattern at a time.

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:13.740
I just want to repeat,
real world use cases

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.780
will clearly be a
combination of these.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.550
And when you look
at Syarif's talk,

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.160
that actually becomes
very apparent.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.950
And before we move on,
just one piece of advice.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.852
Always archive the raw data.

00:07:25.852 --> 00:07:29.040
With stream processing,
mistakes will happen.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.610
And often, mistakes happen
because of errors in user code.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:34.290
And if you've not
archived the data,

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.302
it can become
difficult to recover.

00:07:36.302 --> 00:07:38.010
But if you've archived
the data, then you

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.590
can always backfill recover
by running a batch job

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:41.160
and so forth.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.989
So just a rule of thumb, always
be safe, archive the raw data.

00:07:44.989 --> 00:07:47.280
And of course, you don't need
to keep the archived data

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:48.040
for years on end.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.456
Keep it for a few
months, and then

00:07:49.456 --> 00:07:51.810
when you know that
things have worked fine,

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:53.780
you can discard
the archive data.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:57.630
And often, archiving the
data has very little cost.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:58.130
All right.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.130
So with that, let's dive
into the first pattern.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:04.120
The very first pattern I want
to talk about is very simple.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.700
It just entails reading the data
streams, events that come in,

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.000
and writing them
directly into BigQuery.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.120
BigQuery has an API called
the BigQuery Streaming API.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.190
It's a high throughput
streaming API.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.650
And as soon as you write
an event into BigQuery

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:23.360
through this API, any SQL
query issued to BigQuery

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.800
thereafter will
have results that

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.610
incorporate these new events.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:30.510
So in many cases, to
do real-time processing

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:34.740
of data, all you have to do is
stream the data into BigQuery

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.230
as it comes in.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.330
Of course, that
won't always work.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:43.830
This works when your data
comes in a JSON format.

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:46.500
And often, you want the
fields of the records

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.570
to match the columns
of your table.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.902
There are non-trivial use
cases where that is the case.

00:08:50.902 --> 00:08:52.110
You have JSON data coming in.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:53.610
It maps to the
fields of your table.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.690
You just write it in,
and you're good to go.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.540
And often, real-time
stream processing

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:58.710
is as simple as that.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.590
And I did mention,
this is pragmatic talk.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:05.431
I'm not always going to get
into a sophisticated stream

00:09:05.431 --> 00:09:05.930
processing.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:07.740
And in many cases,
this is all you need,

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:08.937
and you're good to go.

00:09:08.937 --> 00:09:11.270
But of course, that's not
going to be true all the time.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.730
And in most cases,
you will need to do

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.680
some processing of
the data, because it

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.660
comes in a raw format.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.720
And you want to do some
transformations to convert it

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:23.960
from whatever raw format
it came in to some slightly

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:26.750
structured format that
is amenable for analysis.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.150
In many cases, you will
get malformed data,

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:31.770
and you want to filter
out the malformed data.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.770
As you're doing the
transformation and cleaning,

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:35.630
you might convert
data from strings

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:38.420
to more structured
fields, like date time

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.790
fields and [INAUDIBLE] fields
and [? flows ?] and so forth.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:43.320
So you do that in
Cloud Dataflow.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:46.250
In Cloud Dataflow, you
can take the incoming data

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.454
and process each
event one by one.

00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:50.870
So over here, we're talking
about something fairly simple,

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:52.664
where you're not looking
at multiple events

00:09:52.664 --> 00:09:53.330
and aggregation.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:57.800
You're actually looking at
each event in isolation.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.350
And of course, in many
cases, the events coming in

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:02.930
are not going to have
all the information

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.070
you need to do your analysis.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:07.100
So what you need to
do, in many cases,

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:11.030
is join the incoming data
with relevant metadata that

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:12.630
will be in other data stores.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:15.380
And common data stores
that hold metadata might

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.800
be in some BigQuery table.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.400
It could be in Bigtable,
or Spanner, or even

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.680
other relational
databases like Cloud SQL.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:27.770
So you join the incoming
data with relevant metadata

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.590
from other data stores, so
you have enriched your data.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:33.020
And now, you have your
transformed, clean, filtered,

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.360
enriched data, you
write it into BigQuery,

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:37.460
and you're ready for analysis.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:40.610
Now I do want to highlight
that systems like Dataflow,

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:44.270
the SDKs provide very
easy to use operators.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.430
So you merely invoke
the operator [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:48.740
transformations, or
filtering, or joins.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.440
And often, you actually have
to provide just a small snippet

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:53.210
of code that
contains your custom

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:56.460
logic that determines what you
want to do with the events.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.432
So the SDKs make all
of this very easy.

00:10:58.432 --> 00:11:00.890
And of course, these systems
are integrated, so moving data

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:03.470
from one place to the other
is also incredibly easy,

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:06.270
thanks to the pre-built APIs.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.970
Now let's talk about
business use cases.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.530
When it comes to writing data
to BigQuery in real time,

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:14.340
this is applicable across
industries and business use

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:15.220
cases.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.700
This is applicable
in any situation

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:20.430
where you're using
metrics and dashboards

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.350
to run your business,
which increasingly

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:24.480
is most businesses.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.790
In many cases, you just
stream the data in,

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:29.210
and you now are
having dashboards.

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:31.770
Or when you issue a
BI query, the results

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:34.080
reflect what happened now.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:36.600
They're not reflecting
what happened yesterday.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:39.240
And I want to contrast this
with what happens traditionally,

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:42.290
where you might have a
daily job, an ETL job--

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:44.502
ETL is the term commonly used.

00:11:44.502 --> 00:11:46.460
You have an ETL job that's
taking your raw data

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:48.668
and doing a whole bunch of
transformations in a batch

00:11:48.668 --> 00:11:51.580
format, and then loading it
into your analytic warehouse.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:53.020
Now, don't get me wrong.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:55.140
There will still be use
cases where you need

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:56.340
to do that batch processing.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.665
Because whatever
analysis you're doing in

00:11:58.665 --> 00:12:01.800
that job may not be amenable
for a streaming pattern.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.200
But if you look
at your ETL jobs,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.570
you'll find that a significant
chunk of those jobs

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:08.790
can be converted
to streaming jobs.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.130
And if you convert them
to a streaming job,

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.560
dashboards that are
powered by that data

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:17.370
reflect the state of the world
as of now, not as of yesterday,

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.410
or as of last week.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:20.350
Very cool.

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:22.980
So now, let's look at a slightly
more sophisticated pattern,

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:25.313
where you're actually looking
at a whole bunch of events

00:12:25.313 --> 00:12:26.160
together.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.306
In many scenarios,
what you're interested,

00:12:28.306 --> 00:12:30.930
particularly when you're talking
about real-time analysis, what

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:34.560
you're interested in is what
happened in a very recent,

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.050
or the most recent
window of time.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.060
What you're interested
in knowing is,

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:41.550
what happened in the
most recent 30 seconds,

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:43.420
or the most recent
say, five minutes,

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:44.670
or maybe even couple of hours.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.070
But the key thing is you
want to know what happened

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:49.410
in a recent window of time.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:54.330
And as time proceeds, you want
to constantly look and analyze

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.580
what happened within
the most recent window.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:59.007
And to do that, you use
fixed or sliding windows.

00:12:59.007 --> 00:13:00.840
There's a subtle
difference between the two.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.670
Fixed windows don't overlap.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.540
So one window ends, and
the next window begins.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.790
And then with sliding
windows, you actually

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.520
have a large window that
moves a little bit every time.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:14.490
So there is overlap
across subsequent windows.

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:18.540
And depending on your use case,
you can use one or the other.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:22.230
Essentially, you pick the
type of window you want.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:24.086
You invoke the window
within your code,

00:13:24.086 --> 00:13:25.710
and you provide
another snippet of code

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:29.580
that determines how you want
to process the events that

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:31.350
fit into a particular window.

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:34.170
And then the framework
essentially handles the rest.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:37.110
The framework will handle
the creation of these windows

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.469
and the collection of the
events that fit into a window,

00:13:39.469 --> 00:13:41.260
and essentially it will
keep track of time.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:43.320
And as soon as a
window completes,

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.900
as soon as it completes, it'll
call your snippet of code,

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.360
analyze the events that
fell into that window.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.850
So the framework simplifies
this windowing process

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:51.920
significantly.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:54.480
So now let's look at use cases
that are enabled by this.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.340
And there are a whole
bunch of use cases.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.347
Let me start with
retail and e-commerce,

00:13:58.347 --> 00:14:00.180
because they often tend
to be use cases that

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:04.050
are easy to understand
and empathize with.

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:06.840
So with e-commerce,
let's say you want

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:11.460
to keep track of popular items.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:14.220
You want to know items
that are becoming popular

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:18.510
as soon as that trend
starts to surface.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:20.760
And you may not really
care about the popularity

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:22.740
of that item last
week or yesterday,

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.750
because you want to
catch trends early.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.790
So what you do is you
keep track of the searches

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:29.370
that people are issuing
in the e-commerce website,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.830
and you're analyzing
the searches

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.470
that came in within, say,
the past couple of hours.

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:37.440
And then, you know the
products that are popular now.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.870
And then you can match that
with your inventory to know

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.611
if, let's just say, the
inventory cannot keep up with

00:14:42.611 --> 00:14:43.110
the demand.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:45.910
Or if demand is higher
than the inventory,

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:48.000
then you can do dynamic pricing.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.540
Similar use cases where you
might have some product quality

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:54.631
issues, a new batch
of products came in,

00:14:54.631 --> 00:14:55.755
and they have some defects.

00:14:55.755 --> 00:14:57.360
And now you're
seeing a high number

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.010
of complaints or returns.

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.267
And we want to capture
that very quickly.

00:15:01.267 --> 00:15:02.850
And when you're
capturing this, you're

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:06.150
not really interested in the
number of returns or complaints

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.160
that came in last week.

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:10.530
You want to know what happened
in the past, say, few hours.

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.860
And again, fixed and
sliding windows enabled.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:15.510
There are similar use cases
in IT operations, where you're

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:19.132
tracking metrics, and you can
see how that metric is moving

00:15:19.132 --> 00:15:20.340
over a recent window of time.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:24.240
And you can also contrast that
with historical information.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.610
It's also particularly
common in finance,

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:28.494
where for stock
trading strategies,

00:15:28.494 --> 00:15:29.910
a lot of their
strategies actually

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:32.567
look at multiple windows
of time simultaneously,

00:15:32.567 --> 00:15:34.650
because they want to know
how the stock is moving.

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:36.630
So a bunch of use
cases that leverage

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:37.980
fixed and sliding windows.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.507
One more use case
before we move on.

00:15:42.507 --> 00:15:44.340
There are certain use
cases where you're not

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:46.290
necessarily interested
in what happened

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:47.532
in the most recent window.

00:15:47.532 --> 00:15:48.990
What you want to
do is you actually

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:51.250
want to do some
smoothing and denoising.

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:52.920
And this is particularly
true in IoT,

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.010
where you have a lot
of cheap sensors,

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:56.760
and these sensors are
sending information.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:16:00.280
But the information coming
in is often very noisy.

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:01.700
There's a whole bunch of noise.

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:03.930
And you don't want to be
making big decisions based

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:06.510
on the value of
one censor reading.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:09.060
So an easy way to deal
with that is you define

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.290
a reasonable window of time.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.000
And you just average
out, say, the sensor

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:13.200
values within that window.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:17.040
So that essentially serves as a
simple denoising and smoothing

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.590
technique, which is
common in IoT use cases,

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:23.485
and it's also applied in
similar other use cases.

00:16:23.485 --> 00:16:23.985
Very cool.

00:16:23.985 --> 00:16:27.240
So moving on, now let's talk
about a fairly distinct,

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.040
but very popular
type of windowing

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:30.840
called session windowing.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:34.680
And this is useful
for consumer internet

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.180
and mobile applications.

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:39.720
Essentially, what
session windows

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:43.110
are used for is collecting
your click logs.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.892
All the activity that users
perform on the web application,

00:16:45.892 --> 00:16:47.850
or the mobile application,
all that information

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:48.870
is streaming in.

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:51.930
And what you want to do is
isolate each user session

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.724
so you can analyze what
happened within that session.

00:16:54.724 --> 00:16:56.890
So this is different from
fixed and sliding windows,

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:58.920
because the windows
are not fixed.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:02.470
And also, you have a
separate window per user.

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:04.890
So all you have to do
is, in the framework,

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.750
you just have to
mention, what is

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.060
the field in your
incoming events

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:11.310
that corresponds to the user ID?

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:13.950
And then the framework will
automatically split the events

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:15.720
based on that user ID.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.000
And then you have to pass
another parameter, which

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:21.920
is the duration
of inactivity that

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:24.109
signifies the end of a session.

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:27.170
Because there are many ways
you know a session has ended.

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:29.270
The most common way tends
to be, OK, the person

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:31.760
is no longer active
on my application,

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:33.560
so lets consider the
session to be done.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:35.060
So you specify these
two parameters.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:37.340
What is the key that
specifies the user ID?

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.560
And what is the
duration of inactivity

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:42.020
that signifies the
end of a session.

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:43.670
And the framework
handles the rest.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.260
The framework will
take these events,

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:47.350
split these events
by ID, determine

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:50.370
when a window has ended, and as
soon as that window has ended,

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:52.370
it will invoke your
snippet of code.

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:53.840
And no surprises here.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.470
This is used by consumer
internet mobile applications

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:00.740
to understand how
users are navigating

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:01.850
through that application.

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:04.010
Because what you
are able to do is

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:06.650
get the events that
fell within a session,

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:10.280
so you can actually understand
the fact that the user came in,

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:12.230
may have issued a search
query, then clicked

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:14.390
on a certain button,
stayed on this certain page

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.070
for a certain duration of
time, and so on and so forth.

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:20.150
So you analyze each session,
write the results to BigQuery,

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:22.640
and now you actually have
an aggregated view of how

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:25.232
users navigated through
your application.

00:18:25.232 --> 00:18:27.440
And this is a very common
use case, particularly when

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.523
you are deploying new
versions of your application

00:18:29.523 --> 00:18:30.950
or you're doing A/B testing.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.782
You want to identify
mistakes soon.

00:18:33.782 --> 00:18:35.990
You want to identify something
you might have shipped

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.620
that is leading to a significant
drop in a certain usage

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:40.940
pattern that is critical.

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.440
So as soon as you
run your A/B test,

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.606
you have your streaming job
analyzing user sessions,

00:18:44.606 --> 00:18:46.650
identify issues quickly,
and maybe roll back.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.200
It's a very common use, and
a fairly distinct use case

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:51.590
that was worth calling out.

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:53.030
So that was windowing.

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:56.960
So moving along, now,
let's talk about a topic

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:00.470
that is likely to be popular,
GCP and Google Cloud Next.

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:03.950
Machine learning is clearly
a theme of the conference.

00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:07.040
And in many scenarios, you
want to apply machine learning

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:08.410
within a streaming context.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:10.580
Actually, a quick show of hands.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:12.800
I am curious to know how
many folks in the audience

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.750
are interested in
applying machine

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:17.850
learning on streaming data?

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:18.390
All right.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:20.870
That's as I suspected.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:22.310
There's a lot of interest.

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:25.340
Now, in this
particular scenario,

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:27.840
I'm talking about users
training their own custom

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:29.270
models for their use case.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:31.160
And that training,
that heavy lifting,

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:34.494
happens in a batch
format, and it happens not

00:19:34.494 --> 00:19:35.660
within the streaming engine.

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:38.567
It'll happen in something like,
Cloud Machine Learning CMLE,

00:19:38.567 --> 00:19:40.400
which is Cloud Machine
Learning Engine, that

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:42.320
lets you train your own models.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:44.460
In this particular diagram,
I'm using TensorFlow,

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:46.790
because that was the
most recognizable logo

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:49.020
that I could use to
signify machine learning.

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:51.820
But, of course, you can train
other modules, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:53.390
There are a bunch
of other frameworks

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:54.800
that let you do
machine learning.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:56.902
Of course, the heavy
lifting is happening

00:19:56.902 --> 00:19:58.860
where you're training a
machine learning model.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:02.000
But once it's trained, you can
leverage that trained model

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:03.560
within your
streaming application

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:05.840
and to do inference or
scoring on the events

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:07.250
as they stream in.

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:09.440
Now, of course, before
you can do the scoring--

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:11.450
and I want to
reiterate the fact here

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:13.670
that a real-world
use case will employ

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:17.460
a bunch of other patterns
that we mentioned.

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:21.920
Often, before you can pass the
data to the machine learning

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:24.710
model, you'll be doing a whole
bunch of transformations.

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:27.800
Joining and enriching the
data from external metastores

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:29.962
is very common, because
you want to fetch features.

00:20:29.962 --> 00:20:32.170
You do the transformation,
create the feature vector,

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:34.700
pass it to the
model for scoring.

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:38.000
Now some of you may be thinking
that this doesn't sound

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:41.660
very remarkable because
it's rather simple to take

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:43.580
a machine learning
model and invoke it

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:45.590
within the context of a
streaming application.

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:46.656
And that is true.

00:20:46.656 --> 00:20:48.030
As an architectural
pattern, it's

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:50.170
not tremendously remarkable.

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:53.120
But I wanted to reaffirm the
fact that this is happening.

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:54.866
This is a very common pattern.

00:20:54.866 --> 00:20:56.990
And it's actually a very
easy to implement pattern.

00:20:56.990 --> 00:20:58.790
And often, withing your
new streaming application,

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:01.332
you can constantly be checking
to see if a new model has been

00:21:01.332 --> 00:21:03.123
produced, and when a
new model is produced,

00:21:03.123 --> 00:21:04.300
you take the new model.

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:06.669
Or often, customers even
run some A/B testing,

00:21:06.669 --> 00:21:08.210
where they're using
a bunch of models

00:21:08.210 --> 00:21:10.335
to score different events
and see what's happening.

00:21:10.335 --> 00:21:13.040
I just wanted to reaffirm that
this is a very easy pattern

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:14.960
to implement with GCP
tools, particularly

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:17.585
when you're using something like
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:21:17.585 --> 00:21:20.660
And customers are doing it--

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:22.670
increasingly common
pattern, and a lot more

00:21:22.670 --> 00:21:25.610
customers are doing it.

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:27.410
So let's look at
a few use cases.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:29.970
E-commerce and finance,
real-time fraud detection.

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:33.015
That's sort of the poster child
for real-time stream processing

00:21:33.015 --> 00:21:33.890
and machine learning.

00:21:33.890 --> 00:21:35.800
So I actually won't
beat that horse anymore.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:36.837
I'll move on.

00:21:36.837 --> 00:21:37.920
There are other use cases.

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:39.380
For example,
digital advertising,

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:42.677
where you have all the click
and impression information

00:21:42.677 --> 00:21:43.760
streaming in all the time.

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:45.134
And you can use
that in real time

00:21:45.134 --> 00:21:47.630
to determine what
ads have the highest

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:48.860
proclivity of being clicked.

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:51.570
So as the data streams in, you
apply a machine learning model,

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:53.360
and you can determine
what is the right ad

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:56.510
to show for subsequent
users that come in.

00:21:56.510 --> 00:21:59.090
Now, these are all,
again, fairly, I

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:03.497
guess, relatively
known use cases,

00:22:03.497 --> 00:22:05.830
because digital advertising
has been around for a while.

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:08.360
And particularly,
it's gratifying

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:10.460
to talk about use
cases in health care,

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:14.210
where literally lives
are being saved.

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:18.230
A very commonly
referenced use case

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:21.620
is one of doing
sepsis identification.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:25.010
Sepsis is a condition
that happens after fairly

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:26.280
invasive operations.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:27.860
It's a type of infection.

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:30.710
And historically,
nurses would have

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:32.540
to keep checking
in on the patient

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:34.460
periodically to see how
they're doing, to see

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:35.750
if they're at risk for sepsis.

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:39.290
But now, because patients are
connected to various devices

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:41.780
and you're monitoring their
vital statistics, as that data

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:43.070
streams in, you
can apply a machine

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:44.444
learning model
that can determine

00:22:44.444 --> 00:22:45.860
who's at risk for sepsis.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:48.290
And if a patient is
identified, a pager alert

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:49.210
can go out to a nurse.

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:51.800
So the nurses don't have to
proactively keep checking,

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:55.070
and you can offer
more proactive care

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:57.050
to the post-operative patients.

00:22:57.050 --> 00:22:59.780
It was a particularly gratifying
use case to talk about.

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:02.945
Now, in the previous example,
the user had to build a machine

00:23:02.945 --> 00:23:03.570
learning model.

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:05.540
But that's not always true.

00:23:05.540 --> 00:23:08.240
Again, at Next, you
might have visited

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:10.790
a bunch of talks, where we
spoke about the machine learning

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:13.270
APIs that do the machine
learning for you.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:15.312
You give us the data, the
images, or the speech,

00:23:15.312 --> 00:23:17.270
and so forth, and we
apply the machine learning

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:18.440
and return the results.

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:22.310
And invoking these APIs
from a streaming application

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:26.430
is, again, an increasingly
common pattern.

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:28.280
And again, it may
seem unremarkable,

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:29.600
because, again, the heavy
lifting is happening

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:30.650
in the machine learning APIs.

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:31.566
That is actually true.

00:23:31.566 --> 00:23:34.160
This is, come to think of
it, a fairly easy pattern

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:36.920
to implement, but it's an
increasingly common pattern.

00:23:36.920 --> 00:23:38.240
And just one piece of advice.

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:41.390
When you're calling these
APIs, for proper performance,

00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:43.430
you want to limit
the amount of data

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:44.912
that's going over the network.

00:23:44.912 --> 00:23:46.370
So commonly, what
people will do is

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:48.590
they'll use a fixed window,
or even a sliding window,

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:50.390
but often, a fixed
window, just to get

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:52.812
a group of few [INAUDIBLE],,
and then make batch

00:23:52.812 --> 00:23:53.520
calls to the API.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.810
So rather, make calls to the
APIs with a batch of data,

00:23:56.810 --> 00:23:59.690
rather than calling the
API each individual time.

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:01.550
But again, this is a
very simple pattern,

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:04.300
but it enables some fairly
compelling use cases.

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:05.709
And let me walk through these.

00:24:05.709 --> 00:24:08.000
There's one use case-- and
these are actually use cases

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:09.333
that customers have implemented.

00:24:09.333 --> 00:24:11.240
There's one use
case where, if you

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:13.520
have multiplayer
online sessions,

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:16.287
this is where a bunch of folks
are wearing their headsets

00:24:16.287 --> 00:24:18.620
and microphones and they're
playing, say, a first person

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:20.810
shooter, or "World of
Warcraft," or what have you,

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:22.160
they're constantly
talking to each other,

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:23.785
and they're playing
against each other.

00:24:23.785 --> 00:24:27.140
Sometimes, these sessions
actually turn verbally abusive.

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:28.400
This is a common problem.

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:30.940
And now, with the
speech to text APIs,

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:34.010
as that speech information
comes in, you invoke the API.

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:35.390
You convert that speech to text.

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:36.848
And when you convert
that text, you

00:24:36.848 --> 00:24:40.250
apply NLP techniques to identify
when it's becoming abusive.

00:24:40.250 --> 00:24:43.190
And then you can take corrective
action in that session.

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:46.400
Another use case is finance
where, for trading strategies,

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:49.400
you want to consume
the world's newest

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:54.080
information about various
organizations of the world.

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:56.180
Thomson Reuters, for
example, serves a whole bunch

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:57.290
of financial news.

00:24:57.290 --> 00:24:59.430
You want to consume that
news information, invoke

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:02.300
NLP APIs to identify the
entities mentioned in the news

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.732
articles, and also the
sentiment of the news article,

00:25:04.732 --> 00:25:06.940
and that is a rich source
of information to determine

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:08.910
trading strategies.

00:25:08.910 --> 00:25:12.230
Another example,
which is, I guess,

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:16.790
a de jure example, in today's
world, is for home security,

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:20.850
where you have your security
camera feeds coming in,

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:24.470
and you can invoke Vision
APIs to identify entities

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:27.440
in the image frames,
and you can use

00:25:27.440 --> 00:25:30.410
that to dynamically
determine when there might

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:32.900
be a physical threat outside
that security cameras,

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:34.889
outside your home, and so forth.

00:25:34.889 --> 00:25:35.430
So very cool.

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:38.740
Simple pattern, but
very useful pattern.

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:40.520
Now, the last pattern
I want to talk about

00:25:40.520 --> 00:25:43.070
is a slightly more sophisticated
pattern than the other ones

00:25:43.070 --> 00:25:44.360
we've discussed so far.

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:46.190
It's called stateful
streaming, and this

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:49.190
is a pattern where,
as the events flow by,

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:52.917
you're maintaining some state
information about the entities

00:25:52.917 --> 00:25:55.250
and your events, and you're
using that state information

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:57.642
to make some business decision.

00:25:57.642 --> 00:25:59.600
And, again, the key thing
here is the framework

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:00.980
really simplifies this.

00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:04.880
It gives you APIs where, based
on a key such as a user ID,

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:08.180
you can add state information,
and that information

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:09.140
is kept in memory.

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:10.460
You can add state information.

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:12.592
You can read the state
information and take action,

00:26:12.592 --> 00:26:14.300
or you can update that
state information.

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:16.716
So you as a user often just
have to write snippets of code

00:26:16.716 --> 00:26:20.510
to do the look up and
update operations.

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:22.280
And then the framework
handles the rest.

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:24.071
The framework will make
sure that the state

00:26:24.071 --> 00:26:26.270
information-- and it can
be arbitrary information.

00:26:26.270 --> 00:26:29.600
It's up to the application
to decide what is maintained.

00:26:29.600 --> 00:26:31.780
The framework will make
sure that this information

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:34.120
is maintained in
memory and is available

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:35.870
in a performant fashion.

00:26:35.870 --> 00:26:37.900
And these are often
distributed applications.

00:26:37.900 --> 00:26:41.410
So it'll make sure that
full tolerance is covered.

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:43.900
If a VM goes down, for
example, the state information

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:44.440
isn't lost.

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:45.981
The framework will
take care of that.

00:26:45.981 --> 00:26:49.480
So you, as a user, just have
to focus on your look up update

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:51.450
operations often.

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:53.800
So let me start with one
use case where this is used.

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:57.340
The use case is e-commerce,
where you have click events,

00:26:57.340 --> 00:26:59.920
and events that tell you
about the activity of the user

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:01.330
on the e-commerce website.

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:03.040
As these events come
by, you can keep

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:04.970
information-- the
user, for example,

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:06.482
searched for these products.

00:27:06.482 --> 00:27:08.440
The user added a certain
product to their cart,

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:09.790
removed a certain
product from their cart,

00:27:09.790 --> 00:27:10.944
and so on, and so forth.

00:27:10.944 --> 00:27:13.360
And based on that information,
as the next event comes in,

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:15.820
you can dynamically
determine how to personalize

00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:17.096
the experience of the user.

00:27:17.096 --> 00:27:18.470
So can you
dynamically determine,

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:20.890
for example, that, hey, the user
removed an item from the cart,

00:27:20.890 --> 00:27:22.780
but is still searching
for similar items.

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:25.709
Let me give them an offer,
or let me dynamically adjust

00:27:25.709 --> 00:27:28.000
the recommendations that are
made for similar products,

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:28.731
and so forth.

00:27:28.731 --> 00:27:31.230
So that's what stateful stream
processing enables, and there

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:32.450
are other use cases as well.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:34.900
For example, we have a
customer in mobile gaming.

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:37.420
They're dynamically customizing
the difficulty level

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:40.300
of the mobile application
when they identify

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:41.710
that a user is getting stuck.

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:44.350
So a whole bunch of
streaming patterns, and we

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:46.870
did a whirlwind tour
of common patterns.

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:49.840
And next, please join
me in welcoming Syarif

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.630
to stage and talk GO-JEK,
and how they're using

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:53.870
real-time stream processing.

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.259
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:55.259 --> 00:27:56.185
Thank you.

00:27:59.426 --> 00:28:01.670
