00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:26.610
have more than 200,000 merchants
in 50 big cities in Indonesia.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:29.970
And we move to our
GO-JEK data growth.

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:32.750
So in BI, these are
our current stats.

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:37.640
In second quarter of 2018,
from the previous month

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:44.280
until this month, our
data growing more than 30%

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:45.470
per month.

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:48.870
And from that data, we're
going to create more than 700

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:50.570
multi-resolution data sets.

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:54.350
And from that, we can create
more than 20,000 data points.

00:29:54.350 --> 00:29:59.000
From our data analysis, they
create the Metabase and Tableau

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:00.320
sheets.

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:04.340
And from there, they create up
more than 13,000 Metabase cards

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:05.660
and Tableau sheets.

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:09.680
And our user, with our
analysts, created--

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:12.810
the user is more than
2,000 at the moment.

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:16.220
And this number is
increasing day by day.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:22.190
It means we need
more big platform

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:24.460
and more scale for data.

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:30.160
So what is the challenge from
the previous architecture

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:31.330
we have?

00:30:31.330 --> 00:30:35.170
Previously, we use
[INAUDIBLE] SQL, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:30:35.170 --> 00:30:37.060
and also the Python script.

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:45.640
And the previous
architecture, we

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:51.070
have a report at day plus
one, so we couldn't identify

00:30:51.070 --> 00:30:53.240
the problems at the moment.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:31:02.160
And we also had a concern about
that the processing power,

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:05.970
because data comes in
batch, and it's already big,

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:08.970
so we need the process over
two billion data in one shot,

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:10.650
because it's big.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:14.460
So we need a bigger machine with
bigger RAM, with bigger CPU.

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:18.780
And it takes a long time
to process the data.

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:22.110
And the business needs more
real-time data insights.

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:25.620
So previously, we
use [INAUDIBLE] SQL.

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:27.670
As you may know,
that [INAUDIBLE] SQL

00:31:27.670 --> 00:31:31.230
needs maintenance
daily, weekly, monthly.

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:33.460
At least you have to
archive your data.

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:35.130
You have to
[INAUDIBLE] your data,

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:40.110
and you have to index your
data to remain your query fast.

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:47.070
And you also need to
maintain the [INAUDIBLE] SQL

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:48.430
server itself.

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:52.590
And we also use [INAUDIBLE]
to do our ETL batch,

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:59.580
which [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:01.020
Then, what's next?

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:04.140
We move to our GCP platform.

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:07.290
And what we want is we want
high performance scalability

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:10.380
with minimal operational
maintenance using Dataflow,

00:32:10.380 --> 00:32:13.010
with more granular
data with high velocity

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:15.180
and less latency using
stream processing.

00:32:15.180 --> 00:32:17.400
And we want the ability
to solve these problems

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:19.230
with real-time data insights.

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:23.100
So at the moment, we use
Stackdriver to monitor or log

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:24.690
jobs and the machine.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:27.390
And we use TCS our data lake.

00:32:27.390 --> 00:32:31.170
We use BigQuery as our
analytical purpose database.

00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:34.180
We use Pub/Sub to deliver
our stream message.

00:32:34.180 --> 00:32:39.490
And we use Dataflow for our
streaming data pipeline.

00:32:39.490 --> 00:32:41.340
So we move to the use case.

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:43.380
Before we move to
the use case, I

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:46.720
want to show you about
the GO-JEK goals.

00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:48.300
So what is GO-JEK goals?

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:51.160
The GO-JEK goals is on
the North Star metric.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:53.080
So what is North Star metric?

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:54.750
North Star metric is
something that you

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:57.510
want to align with the
organization behind,

00:32:57.510 --> 00:32:59.910
so that [INAUDIBLE] knows
what company goal is.

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:03.610
So at GO-JEK that would be
complete booking transactions.

00:33:03.610 --> 00:33:05.760
So what is metrics
that matter when

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:08.850
we want to increase the
completed booking transactions?

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:12.900
We have also to increase
the total bookings.

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:16.290
We have also [INAUDIBLE]
the allocation

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:18.720
and reduce the
cancellation rate.

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:22.410
Total bookings is
the bookings that

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:24.990
get created by the
customer, and the allocation

00:33:24.990 --> 00:33:28.050
is something that
we want to align

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:30.870
about the customer
and the driver.

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:35.220
And the cancellation
rate is when

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:39.940
the customer
created the booking,

00:33:39.940 --> 00:33:43.170
the driver couldn't complete it.

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:45.790
And for that use case,
I'll just show you

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:49.290
that when the
allocation is mismatched

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:52.380
between the driver and the
customer, the completed

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:55.730
bookings wouldn't be reached.

00:33:55.730 --> 00:33:59.250
So, how to rebalance
supply and demand?

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:03.010
And how do you view
it in real time?

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:06.660
So this is the GO-JEK
supply-demand use case.

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:09.090
So as a user, I
want to know which

00:34:09.090 --> 00:34:12.179
location have mismatched
levels of supply and demand

00:34:12.179 --> 00:34:13.409
in real time.

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:15.960
And I want to know the
particular drivers are

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:18.210
based on the real-time
data aggregation.

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:21.120
So if you see the
map in front of you,

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:25.260
if I hover our pointer in
the small dots with just

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:30.949
the driver, I can know driver
[INAUDIBLE] information

00:34:30.949 --> 00:34:34.080
regarding the name, the
plate number, and also

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:36.159
the rating of the driver itself.

00:34:36.159 --> 00:34:38.610
And I want to be able to
identify driver in low demand

00:34:38.610 --> 00:34:42.179
area to move to
high demand area.

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:47.610
So after discussion, this is
the pipeline we already made.

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:53.199
And as you know, we have
so many pipeline here.

00:34:53.199 --> 00:34:55.449
We have three big
pipelines in here.

00:34:55.449 --> 00:34:57.730
The one is stream
booking count pipeline.

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:00.205
The second one is dimension
pipeline from the BigQuery

00:35:00.205 --> 00:35:02.680
And stream driver
location pipeline,

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:05.920
we got from that Pub/Sub driver
location log or the driver

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:08.560
ping.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:10.290
For your information,
our driver ping

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:14.800
is created every 10 seconds.

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:19.500
It means in that day, we have
more than 10 billion data

00:35:19.500 --> 00:35:21.080
pings.

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:24.590
So from streaming
booking pipeline,

00:35:24.590 --> 00:35:29.050
we can assume from our Pub/Sub
booking log, our messages.

00:35:29.050 --> 00:35:32.935
And we created a model, which
by grouped the [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:32.935 --> 00:35:35.190
ID, and also the status.

00:35:35.190 --> 00:35:37.420
And we used sliding
window as animation

00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:38.980
before the sliding window.

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:42.445
We are only concerned
about the current window.

00:35:42.445 --> 00:35:46.960
And we filter with
[INAUDIBLE] status.

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:49.930
And the dimension pipeline,
we query from BigQuery

00:35:49.930 --> 00:35:54.310
we go from tree table, location
table, service [INAUDIBLE],,

00:35:54.310 --> 00:35:56.340
and also the driver profile.

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:59.290
Each of them, we have
created each model,

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:02.380
and we'll refresh
our window per day.

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:05.680
And on stream driver location
pipeline, we [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:05.680 --> 00:36:07.930
from Pub/Sub driver
location log.

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:11.830
And we created a model,
and we use fixed window.

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:14.780
And after that, all of
them will be joined,

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:18.610
and will be stored to our
stream log memory store,

00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:21.760
and our API we will call
the stream memory store,

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:25.420
and we will create
the mapping from this.

00:36:25.420 --> 00:36:27.790
And we move to implementation.

00:36:27.790 --> 00:36:31.990
For the implementation, we only
focus about the how we could.

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:37.570
So we just got the pipeline
using the Apache Beam SDK.

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:41.640
And we deployed it to the
Dataflow, and that's it.

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:45.670
We didn't spin up any machine,
any cluster, or Stackdriver.

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:48.400
What we have to do is
just push the [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:51.220
to the Apache Beam, and
Apache Beam will do the rest,

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:54.810
like creating the
machine, and create

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:56.980
the pipeline view
for us, and also

00:36:56.980 --> 00:37:00.310
the Stackdriver by default.

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:03.010
And this, the pipeline
implementations

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:05.950
that already happened.

00:37:05.950 --> 00:37:09.490
As you can see, this
is autoscale capability

00:37:09.490 --> 00:37:15.920
of data, which means that
Dataflow can scale from one

00:37:15.920 --> 00:37:19.430
worker to several worker,
based on how big your data

00:37:19.430 --> 00:37:21.080
and how big your processing is.

00:37:23.700 --> 00:37:25.640
And this is the scatter
map implementation

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:27.510
is fast forwarded.

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:31.140
Actually, it's refreshed
in every minute.

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:37.630
So the idea of the [? inside ?]
in here [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:41.670
don't last long, which is
good, because the booking might

00:37:41.670 --> 00:37:45.240
be big in this area,
but not for a long time,

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:49.050
because the driver took
the order immediately,

00:37:49.050 --> 00:37:53.820
so the aggregated booking that
created it will be disappearing

00:37:53.820 --> 00:37:57.080
in there after several minutes.

00:37:57.080 --> 00:38:01.130
But that is not enough.

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:05.660
How, if in the practical
area, there also canceled

00:38:05.660 --> 00:38:08.140
booking happened in that day?

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:11.160
So if there are
many bookings, it

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:12.990
can be completed or canceled.

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:16.210
How can we make sure that
every booking is completed,

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:17.665
not cancelled?

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:25.190
So that's why we created
extended use cases.

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:29.980
And this is our
extended use cases.

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:31.740
Cancellation alert use case--

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:37.190
previously, we got this chart
the day after, at day plus one,

00:38:37.190 --> 00:38:40.880
and we couldn't see
that, oh, there's

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:42.350
something happened yesterday.

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:45.110
There's a spike of
cancellation yesterday.

00:38:45.110 --> 00:38:47.540
But why was that happen?

00:38:47.540 --> 00:38:50.330
That was too late, because
that was yesterday.

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:53.870
And maybe today,
it don't happen.

00:38:53.870 --> 00:38:57.290
So as a user, I want to
be notified when there's

00:38:57.290 --> 00:38:59.660
a huge cancellation
at that time,

00:38:59.660 --> 00:39:01.760
so I can determine
the root cause

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:03.130
and fix it as soon as possible.

00:39:06.820 --> 00:39:08.260
You know this pipeline before.

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:10.390
It's from our main use case.

00:39:10.390 --> 00:39:14.050
From this use case, I want to
show you that in Apache Beam,

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:17.700
we can create [INAUDIBLE]
from this previous pipeline,

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:20.720
so we don't create
pipeline from scratch.

00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:23.400
What we have to do is we have
the concern about the stream

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:26.290
booking count pipeline
only, because we

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:28.990
don't care about the dimension
pipeline and the stream driver

00:39:28.990 --> 00:39:33.410
location pipeline,
so we move it out.

00:39:33.410 --> 00:39:35.630
And after we get
the model, we just

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:37.575
group by booking status
and [INAUDIBLE] ID.

00:39:37.575 --> 00:39:41.780
[INAUDIBLE] to sync
to Pub/Sub downstream,

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:45.170
and will be consumed by
our cancellation pipeline.

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:47.900
And the result
will be like this.

00:39:47.900 --> 00:39:51.110
This is our Stackdriver.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:56.270
For example, if the threshold
of canceled bookings

00:39:56.270 --> 00:39:58.400
is more than 10%
of total booking,

00:39:58.400 --> 00:40:01.580
it will give the alert.

00:40:01.580 --> 00:40:06.560
And after it go
up the threshold,

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:09.230
it will give us the
alert, like this, alert,

00:40:09.230 --> 00:40:11.420
cancelled bookings
more than 10%.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:14.987
But again, that's not enough.

00:40:14.987 --> 00:40:16.070
How do we know the reason?

00:40:16.070 --> 00:40:18.680
After we get the alert, so what?

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:21.160
I couldn't do anything about it.

00:40:21.160 --> 00:40:25.540
We have to know the reason
behind the cancelled booking.

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:29.910
And is there another
use case, again?

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:32.570
So as a user, I want to know
the reason cancelled booking

00:40:32.570 --> 00:40:33.560
in real time.

00:40:33.560 --> 00:40:37.160
And I want to notify
myself and the merchant

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.980
if item maybe sold
out, or maybe the price

00:40:39.980 --> 00:40:44.090
differs with the apps and
the [INAUDIBLE] store,

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:48.470
or maybe store is closed
out of the scheduled.

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:52.310
And this is the new pipeline
that we have created.

00:40:52.310 --> 00:40:54.860
We still consume from
our Pub/Sub booking log.

00:40:54.860 --> 00:40:57.800
We filtered status by
the cancelled only,

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:00.020
and we create the
different model.

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:02.600
We created the group
by [INAUDIBLE] ID

00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:05.630
and the cancelled reason,
and we use fixed window.

00:41:05.630 --> 00:41:08.950
And then we sync to
our Pub/Sub downstream,

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:10.880
and we created
notification system.

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:14.850
And also, this pipeline
can synced to our BigQuery,

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:21.510
so our analysts can determine
their result better.

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:26.760
So this is the
JSON format that we

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:29.470
sync to the Pub/Sub downstream.

00:41:29.470 --> 00:41:33.870
It include the [INAUDIBLE]
ID and the merchant name,

00:41:33.870 --> 00:41:39.450
and also cancel reason, and
how many happened in that day.

00:41:39.450 --> 00:41:42.809
So after the use
cases, three use cases,

00:41:42.809 --> 00:41:45.100
I want to share with you
about the three takeaways that

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:48.870
are [INAUDIBLE]
about using Dataflow.

00:41:48.870 --> 00:41:52.170
With Dataflow, we can create
streaming data processing.

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:55.520
We are able to solve business
with real-time data insight.

00:41:55.520 --> 00:41:58.740
And Dataflow delivers high
performance scalability

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:01.270
with minimal
operational maintenance,

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:03.870
as you already get that.

00:42:03.870 --> 00:42:06.720
Dataflow can scale from
one to several workers,

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:10.470
based on how big your data and
how complex your processing is.

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:12.540
And our report will be
delivered less than a

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:17.640
day, so our analysts
can do more,

00:42:17.640 --> 00:42:20.790
getting more insight
with more recent data.

00:42:20.790 --> 00:42:22.720
And that's all for me.

00:42:22.720 --> 00:42:24.040
Back to you, Anand.

00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:25.872
Thank you very much.

00:42:25.872 --> 00:42:27.756
